  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The rate at which the population of a particular country grows $P(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.02P.\n$$\n\nHow many years does it take for the population to double? Round your answer to the nearest year.",
  "worked_solution": "We model the population growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.02P.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.02t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial population.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the population of the country is double the initial number. That is,\n\n$$\nP({t}_{1})=2{P}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & ={P}_{0}{e}^{0.02\\cdot {t}_{1}} \\\\ 2{P}_{0} & ={P}_{0}{e}^{0.02{t}_{1}} \\\\ 2 & ={e}^{0.02{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u20612 & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{0.02{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u20612 & =0.02{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{0.02} \\\\ {t}_{1} & \u224834.657\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $35$ years for the population to double.",
